affiliation={University of Victoria VictoriaBC, Canada},
abstract={This forum contribution discusses an arts-based research project that reconfigures survivor testimony within a relational ethics framework. Highlighting the creative partnership between Utrecht-based comics artist Tobi Dahmen and his research collaborator Akram, a young man who survived the Syrian prison system, the author proposes to employ comic drawing as an inquiry-based tool and caring practice during collaborative life writing sessions. A commitment to relational storytelling—honouring relationships that are based on mutual trust and shared authority—also fosters a practice of mindful, reflective slow scholarship. Taking time is thus a critical pillar of collaborative storytelling work for the survivors and the artists, as well as all members on the research team, as they mutually negotiate the flow, pace, and rhythm of co-narration. © German Studies Canada, 2025.},
author_keywords={arts-based research;  care ethics;  collaborative life writing;  genocide;  mass atrocity studies;  relational ethics;  slow scholarship;  survivor testimony},
references={This was funded by a SSHRC Partnership Development Grant, p. 2019. , 22; This is funded by a SSHRC Partnership Grant, pp. 2022-2029; As a precautionary measure, we decided to not disclose Akram’s last name. I have permission from Tobi Dahmen, Akram, and Uğur Ümit Üngör to discuss various aspects of their collaboration; We are very grateful to Mitchel Stuffers, research intern at NIOD and master’s student in Holocaust and Genocide studies at Uppsala University, Sweden, for transcribing the interview sessions; Ümit Üngör is the co-lead in the Iraq and Syria research cluster; Dahmen envisions that it will take about six months to bring the digitally drawn graphic novel to completion, , Barring unforeseen circumstances, —Akram’s Story will be published in spring 2026; The sharing of authority, according to Michael Frisch, includes “not only a distribution of knowledge from those who have it to those who do not, but a more profound sharing of knowledges, an implicit and sometimes explicit dialogue from very different vantages about the shape, meaning, and implications of history, , (xxii); Re: Short Essay on Our Research Collaboration, , Received by Charlotte Schallié, 13 Oct. 2024. Email interview; (2023) Interview, , By Tobi Dahmen, 7 June; Bouton, Edith, Blurring the Boundaries: Opening and Sustaining Dialogic Spaces (2024) Theory into Practice, 63 (2), pp. 182-197. , https://doi.org/10.1080/00405841.2024.2307837; Dahmen, Tobi, Akram’s Story, , 12 Oct. 2024, collection of Tobi Dahmen, Utrecht; Dahmen, Tob, (2024) Re: Short Essay on Our Research Collaboration, , Received by Charlotte Schallié, 11 Oct. Email interview; Frank, Arthur W., (1995) The Wounded Storyteller: Body, Illness and Ethics, , U of Chicago; Frisch, Michael, (1990) A Shared Authority: Essays on the Craft and Meaning of Oral and Public History, , SUNY; Narrative Art and Visual Storytelling in Holocaust and Human Rights Education. 2019–22, , https://holocaustgraphicnovels.uvic.ca/, Accessed 2