journal={Journal of Geography in Higher Education},
year={2025},
volume={49},
number={4},
pages={543-556},
doi={10.1080/03098265.2025.2533268},
note={cited By 2},
url={https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105010675573&doi=10.1080%2f03098265.2025.2533268&partnerID=40&md5=3e8026ae896b38f03304ac50e58a1838},
affiliation={Faculty of Spatial Sciences, University College Groningen and Urban and Regional Studies Institute, University of Groningen, Groningen, Netherlands},
abstract={This article discusses the role of affect and emotion in teaching and learning by drawing on the case of an interdisciplinary, arts-based, and participatory educational project, “Break Out”/“Show Yourself”. The case entails a collaboration between people with multiple physical impairments, students from the Geography and Planning degree programme (and later also from the Liberal Arts and Sciences programme) at the University of Groningen, and students from the Art Academy Minerva, also in Groningen. The article draws on a rich data set of student reflections, students’ fieldwork photos and photos of their visual storytelling through knowledge sharing activities. Using an “Affective Pedagogies framework”, the article discusses different spaces in which students engaged in learning about disability and the inclusive city through emotion and affect. It highlights the way in which learning spaces might allow for struggle, joy as well as discomfort, as necessary for learning. © 2025 The Author(s). Published by Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
author_keywords={Affective pedagogies;  arts-based participatory research;  disability;  experiential learning},
keywords={disability;  educational development;  learning;  student;  teaching, Groningen [Groningen (PRV)];  Groningen [Netherlands];  Netherlands},
funding_details={Rijksuniversiteit GroningenRijksuniversiteit Groningen, RUG},
funding_details={Nederlandse Organisatie voor Wetenschappelijk OnderzoekNederlandse Organisatie voor Wetenschappelijk Onderzoek, NWO, 406.22, SW.061},
funding_details={Nederlandse Organisatie voor Wetenschappelijk OnderzoekNederlandse Organisatie voor Wetenschappelijk Onderzoek, NWO},
funding_text 1={This work was supported by the research programme SGW Open Competition M Social Sciences 2022 which is (partly) financed by the Dutch Research Council (NWO). It is part of the project Everyday Geographies of Being and Becoming Disabled (with project number 406.22.SW.061). I am grateful for the participation of all students and clients from \u2018s Heeren Loo in the project discussed in this article, I realise that the project took place outside of their comfort zones, at least initially. Giving permission to use and cite from their reflections is brave and important allowing me to provide a case study that has the power to engage, affect and inspire other educators. I am grateful for being part of the student journey and the realisations and successes that stood at its end. In addition to receiving staff time and insights from our societal part \u2018s Heeren Loo, the project was supported by the Honours College at the University of Groningen as well as the University College Groningen. Andrea Stultiens, my colleague from the Art Academy Minerva, played a significant role in guiding the creative process. Coming from two different institutions with different educational practices, we sometimes muddled through, but her openness and insightfulness helped make the project positively memorable. Arline Dul and Johanna Paschen played an important role in thinking along the developments of the article at different times. Huib Ernste provided the opportunity to present first thoughts on this article during his valedictorian conference.},
