year={2025},
volume={21},
number={2},
pages={293-329},
doi={10.1163/17932548-12341538},
note={cited By 0},
url={https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105021980211&doi=10.1163%2f17932548-12341538&partnerID=40&md5=bfb0f25e69345196a8dd693ed447f345},
affiliation={Department of Visual Communication Design, Faculty of Creative Industries, Telkom University, Bandung, Indonesia; Industrial Engineering Department, Faculty of Industrial Engineering, Telkom University, Bandung, Indonesia},
abstract={This research reinterprets Wacinwa (Wayang China Jawa) – a hybrid Chinese-Javanese shadow puppetry tradition – not as a fixed performance art to be preserved, but as a modular visual storytelling system adaptable to contemporary narratives. Traditionally associated with the tale of Sie Djin Koei, Wacinwa has often been revived through reenactment (Kiswantoro, 2019). In contrast, this study explores how its visual form and construction methods can be reimagined through participatory design and narrative adaptation, using Dendam Membara, a wuxia novel by Kho Ping Hoo. Employing visual research methods and Hutcheon’s theory of adaptation (Hutcheon, 2006) and the ATUMICS framework (Nugraha 2019), the project examines how Wacinwa’s interchangeable puppet structure allows for the reinterpretation of new characters and scenes. Students were invited to design new puppets and compositions based on Kho Ping Hoo’s narratives, emphasizing themes of loyalty, justice, and cultural identity. The findings align with Hutcheon’s framework on visual storytelling, indicating that youth respond more actively when engaging with familiar media narratives and modular design systems. Rather than preserving Wacinwa in its original narrative form, this project proposes an adaptive transformation: repurposing its visual language as a contemporary platform that remains culturally resonant while expanding its narrative scope. © Dimas Krisna Aditya, 2025.},
author_keywords={adaptation theory;  ATUMICS;  Chinese Indonesian culture;  Kho Ping Ho;  Wacinwa;  Wayang},
references={Aditya, D.K., Desintha, S., Lawe, A.R., Gusti, I., Lionardi, A., Kusumawati, I.N., Reintroducing and Preserving Wayang Cina Jawa Using Kho Ping Hoo’s Wu Xia Narration in New Media (2022) International Conference on Chinese Diaspora in Southeast Asia Studies (Socio Cultural Research of the Chinese Diaspora in Southeast Asia), pp. 133-139. , Vongphantuset, Jirawat and Kusbiantoro, Krismanto and Budianto, Pauw and Luli, Qianqian and Koji, Tsuda and Xiaoqiang, Yang,. Bandung: MCU Press. ISBN; Aditya, D.K., Pengaruh Ilustrasi Poster Film Shaw Brothers Pada Ilustrasi Cover Cergam Dan Novel Silat Di Indonesia. (2018) The Influence of Shaw Brothers Film Poster Illustrations on Cover Illustrations for Martial Arts Comics and Novels in Indonesia) Kalatanda: Jurnal Desain Grafis dan Media Kreatif, 1 (2), p. 183; Dermawan, T., Agus, (2016) Melipat Air: Jurus Pendekar Tionghoa: Lee Man Fong, Siauw Tik Kwie, Lin Wasim (Folding Water: Chinese Warriors’ Moves: Lee Man Fong, , Siauw Tik Kwie, Lin Wasim). Jakarta: Kepustakaan Populer Gramedia KPG; Elleström, L., Media Transformation: The Transfer of Media Characteristics among Media (2014) Media Transformation: The Transfer of Media Characteristics Among Media, pp. 1-104. , Palgrave Macmillan, UK; Hawkins, A.M., CreatingthroughDance (1988), pp. 373-393. , NJ: Princeton Book Company. Hutcheon, Linda. 2006. ATheory of Adaptation. New York: Routledge; Kiswantoro, A., Perancangan Wacinwa: Sang Manggalayudha.” (Wacinwa Design: The Manggalayudha), Wayang Nusantara (2019) Journal of Puppetry, 3 (1). , https://journal.isi.ac.id/index.php/wayang/article/view/3055/1203, Accessed on 20 July 2025; Koesoemadinata, M., Pramana, I., Aditya, D.K., Visualizations of Wayang Characters in Comics (Case Study: Bima and Arjuna Characters in the Arts of Ardisoma, Teguh Santosa and Is Yuniarto) (2016) Bandung Creative Movement 2016 | Multidisciplinary Design: Harmonizing Design in Today’s Society, Technology and Business, 3 (1), pp. 15-19. , htt