url={https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105030460863&doi=10.1109%2fINISTA68122.2025.11249525&partnerID=40&md5=23e24b23201a4a51d72147a500aa6de9},
affiliation={French Geological Survey (BRGM), Orléans, France; University of Orléans, Loire Valley Institute for Advanced Studies, Orléans, France; Chitkara University, Institute of Engineering and Technology, Punjab, India},
abstract={Although satellite programs like Copernicus now provide abundant Earth-observation (EO) data, non-specialists still struggle to access, process, and interpret it. This paper introduces a swarm-agent framework that closes this cognitive and technical gap. Plain-language questions are converted into structured workflows executed by specialized, modular agents that follow the four classic data-storytelling stages: clarifying the user's question, exploring the data, structuring the narrative, and presenting the results. The system then generates a concise story, supported by visuals and straightforward explanations. A proof of concept (POC) using Sentinel-5P data demonstrates how this approach can track pollutant levels in France's Centre-Val de Loire region. The POC shows how a data-storytelling-inspired swarm-agent architecture can make EO insights accessible, intelligible, and transparent to a broad audience in under 10seconds. © 2025 IEEE.},
author_keywords={Conversational systems;  Data storytelling;  Earth Observation;  Satellite data;  Swarm agents},
keywords={Intelligent agents;  Remote sensing;  Satellites;  Swarm intelligence;  User profile, Agent Framework;  Conversational systems;  Data storytelling;  Earth observation data;  Earth observations;  Proof of concept;  Satellite data;  Satellite programs;  Swarm agents;  User's profiles, Observatories},
