note={cited By 1},
url={https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85196663147&doi=10.22492%2fijcs.9.si.03&partnerID=40&md5=8b6ac5695dbe216521452d9ab8b23f6c},
affiliation={Beijing Normal University, Hong Kong Baptist University, United International College, Zhuhai, China; Hong Kong Baptist University, Hong Kong},
abstract={AI Amok (2020), directed by Yu Irie, offers an incisive exploration of artificial intelligence’s role in a near-future Japanese society and is set in the year 2030. Employing the analytical framework provided by Bordwell and Thompson’s Film Art: An Introduction, this research explores the film’s narrative construction, visual storytelling techniques, and its thematic depth, particularly focusing on the portrayal and implications of artificial intelligence. Distinct from the often distant futures depicted in science fiction cinema, AI Amok presents a vision of the future that mirrors current technological trajectories, especially in healthcare and urban development. This stands in contrast to films like Blade Runner 2049 (2017) and Her (2013), which envision more abstract futures. AI Amok thus distinguishes itself through a narrative that integrates AI into societal fabrics, confronting the ethical quandaries this integration elicits. The analysis reveals a scenario that feels immediate and tangible, offering a credible sight into the technological advancements and challenges of the near future. Moreover, the study highlights AI Amok’s nuanced depiction of AI’s roles within healthcare and government, suggesting a reflection on and projection of these technologies’ evolving paths. Unlike the overt technologization seen in The Matrix (1999) or Ex Machina (2014), AI Amok opts for more subtle visual effects to convey AI’s ubiquity, providing a unique perspective on AI’s potential to shape societal dynamics. This approach enriches the discourse on science fiction cinema, contributing a distinctive viewpoint to the ongoing debates concerning the ethical development and integration of artificial intelligence in real-world contexts. © 2024, The International Academic Forum (IAFOR). All rights reserved.},
author_keywords={artificial intelligence;  film;  futurism;  Japan;  science fiction;  Yu Irie},
references={Archer, N., Science fiction cinema and the road movie: Case studies in the estranged mobile gaze (2019) Mobilities, Literature, Culture, pp. 281-306. , https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-27072-8_12, Marian Aguilar et al. (eds). Cham: Palgrave; Benson, M., (2018) Space Odyssey: Stanley Kubrick, Arthur C. Clarke, and the making of a masterpiece, , Simon & Schuster; Berghaus, G., Futurism and the technological imagination (2009) Avant-Garde Critical Studies, 24. , https://doi.org/10.1163/9789042027480, Brill; Bordwell, D., Thompson, K., Smith, J., (1993) Film art: An introduction, , New York: McGraw-Hill; Casiddu, N., Porfirione, C., Burlando, F., Vacanti, A., A century of humanoid robotics in cinema: A design-driven review (2021) Advances in Human Factors in Robots, Unmanned Systems and Cybersecurity. AHFE 2021. Lecture Notes in Networks and Systems, 268. , https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-79997-7_13, Zallio, M., Raymundo Ibañez, C., Hernandez, J.H. (eds) Springer; (2020) (Takao Osawa, Kento Kawaku and AI Takano will challenge the company “AI Collapse”), , https://www.cinemacafe.net/article/2018/12/15/59485.html; Charniak, E., (1985) Introduction to artificial intelligence, , Pearson Education India; Coeckelbergh, M., Artificial intelligence: Some ethical issues and regulatory challenges (2019) Technology and regulation, 2019, pp. 31-34; Dinello, D., (2005) Technophobia!: Science fiction visions of posthuman technology, , University of Texas Press; Darke, C., (2005) Alphaville (Jean-Luc Godard, 1965), , University of Illinois Press; Gibbs, J., (2002) Mise-en-scène: Film style and interpretation, 10. , Wallflower Press; Huang, M. F., Analyzing civilization transmission from the perspective of creativity in science fiction films (2021) New Films, 2, pp. 12-18; (2022) AI Amok (AI)-The Japanese film festival Australia, , https://japanesefilmfestival.net/film/ai-amok/, [online]; Jeansonne, C., The future we have already made: Fatalism in Terminator 3: The Rise 